// ⚠⚠ **THE RAW TLS BOUNDARY, AND THE ONLY FUNCTION IN THIS FILE THAT MAY ANSWER `-1`.** Shard id of
// the calling OS thread's adopted P, or -1 when no multi-P scheduler owns this thread. Signature
// stub (returns -1) for targets without a GMP scheduler; x64-Windows and arm64-macOS OVERRIDE THIS
// EXACT LABEL with a TLS-reading machine-code version. ⚠ Both emitters live in **`maxon-selfhosted/`**
// (`emitX64SlabCurrentPId`; `emitArm64SlabCurrentPId` in `Targets/Arm64/Arm64MacosGreenThread.maxon`)
// and NOWHERE ELSE — grepping shv2 or the bootstrap for either name finds nothing, which is
// consistent with this whole file: v1 is the only compiler that ever compiled it. That is why
// neither its `-1` nor its NAME is this file's to change —
// renaming it would leave those emitters registering a label nothing calls and every caller
// resolving to this always-(-1) stub.
//
// ⛔ **NOTHING BUT `__slab_owning_p_tag` MAY CALL IT.** The `-1` is an out-of-band encoding at a
// foreign boundary, so it is converted AT that boundary into an owning-P TAG, and every site
// downstream decodes by EQUALITY against a named tag rather than by asking whether an id is
// negative. That matters twice over: an ordering test against 0 is exactly the shape whose answer
// the signed/unsigned reading changes, while an equality's answer is the same in both.
function __slab_current_p_id() returns ProcIdOrAbsent
	return -1
end '__slab_current_p_id'

// Number of size classes in the `__slab_class_size` table (8/16/24/32/48/64/96/
// 128/192/256/384/512/1024/2048/4096/8192/16384/32768). Every class scan iterates
// exactly this many entries.
let SLAB_NUM_SIZE_CLASSES = 18

// Largest request served from a size class (the last class, index 17). Anything
// bigger routes to the OS-direct path (`__slab_os_direct_alloc`).
let SLAB_MAX_SMALL_ALLOC = 32768

// OS page granularity the OS-direct path aligns allocations up to (8KB), shared by
// `__slab_os_direct_alloc` and `__slab_rounded_size`. (The arena-map index math
// uses its own 13-bit page masks, kept as literals there — a distinct concern.)
let SLAB_PAGE_SIZE = 8192

// Number of mcache shard rows (one per possible P). The mcache base is sized
// SLAB_MAX_SHARDS × SLAB_NUM_SIZE_CLASSES × 8 bytes (see the `__slab_mcache_base`
// data-section entry).
let SLAB_MAX_SHARDS = 256

// Bytes between consecutive mcache rows: one 8-byte span* slot per size class
// (SLAB_NUM_SIZE_CLASSES × 8). Must stay lock-step with the size-class count.
let SLAB_MCACHE_ROW_STRIDE = 144

// ⭐ **THE OWNING-P TAG: what an mspan's owning_p (+0x40) holds.** Either a real P id
// (0 .. num_procs-1) or one of these two tags. Both sit far above SLAB_MAX_SHARDS, so no real P id
// can ever collide with either — the same argument the parked sentinel has always made, now stated
// once for the pair.
//
// ⚠ **THE "NO P" TAG IS NON-NEGATIVE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.** This field is read straight
// out of foreign memory as a raw `MachineWord`, so the old `-1` for a raw-thread-owned span had to
// be decoded as `owningP < 0` — an ORDERING test on an unsigned word, which is always false. The
// raw-owned locking arm in `__slab_free` would simply have stopped firing, silently, over a green
// suite. A non-negative tag makes every decode an `==`, whose answer no reading can change.
let MSPAN_OWNING_P_RAW = 0xFFFFFFFE
let MSPAN_OWNING_P_PARKED = 0xFFFFFFFF

// mspan scavenge state (+0x48), a two-epoch grace guard so the scavenger only
// hands back spans that have stayed fully free across two scavenge passes — the
// active working set (spans freed then reused between passes) is never decommitted,
// which is what prevents decommit/recommit thrash on a busy allocator.
//   ACTIVE      — committed and recently live; a fresh or just-reused span.
//   SEEN_FREE   — observed fully free by one scavenge pass; in its grace period.
//   DECOMMITTED — pages handed back to the OS; must be re-backed + rebuilt on reuse.
let SPAN_SCAV_ACTIVE = 0
let SPAN_SCAV_SEEN_FREE = 1
let SPAN_SCAV_DECOMMITTED = 2

// The mcache row reserved for ALL raw OS threads (the subprocess sync worker, the
// IOCP completion poller — threads with no adopted P, which carry
// MSPAN_OWNING_P_RAW). It MUST be distinct from every real P's row (a real P's row
// is its id, 0..num_procs-1), so a raw thread's lockless-looking fast path never
// shares a cached span with P[0]. Real Ps never reach num_procs == SLAB_MAX_SHARDS
// in practice; the `>= SLAB_MAX_SHARDS` cap in `__slab_shard_for_tag` is for an
// unreachable >256-CPU host and would only collide there. Raw-thread access to this
// row is additionally serialized under __slab_lock (see __slab_alloc / __slab_free),
// since multiple raw threads share it — they have no exclusive-owner guarantee the
// per-P fast path relies on.
let SLAB_RAW_SHARD = SLAB_MAX_SHARDS - 1

// ⭐ **THE ONE CONVERSION SITE.** The owning-P tag for the CALLING thread: its P's id when it has
// adopted one, MSPAN_OWNING_P_RAW when it has not. This is the sole caller of
// `__slab_current_p_id`, and the `-1` stops here.
//
// It answers the tag rather than a (bool, id) PAIR deliberately: the alloc and free paths each need
// BOTH "which row am I" and "which tag do I stamp / compare against", and a pair of queries would
// read the TLS twice per allocation — twice per FREE too — where one read serves both. It would also
// be able to straddle an adopt/release between the two reads and answer about two different Ps.
// One read, one value, and every consumer below is pure arithmetic on it.
function __slab_owning_p_tag() returns MachineWord
