// Reset a just-reclaimed span's scavenge grace state to ACTIVE and, if the
// scavenger actually handed its pages back to the OS (DECOMMITTED), re-back and
// re-zero them before the owning P allocates from it — the free-list head/links
// lived in the discarded data pages. A span merely in its grace period (SEEN_FREE)
// still has an intact free list and bump cursor, so it just gets the reset. Caller
// holds __slab_lock, so this cannot race a concurrent scavenge of the same span.
//
// The memzero here is Go's `needZeroAfterSysUnused()`, and we take the always-true
// branch. Recommitted pages are NOT reliably zero:
//
//   Windows  VirtualAlloc(MEM_COMMIT) over a decommitted range -> zero
//   Linux    madvise(MADV_DONTNEED)   -> refaults zero
//   macOS    madvise(MADV_FREE)       -> NOT zero; the kernel may hand the SAME
//                                        page back with its old contents intact
//   wasm     decommit is a no-op      -> contents survive verbatim
//
// Go's own docs say the same of this transition: "Accessing memory in such a region
// is undefined (may fault, may give back unexpected zeroes, etc.)". We zero eagerly
// rather than carrying a per-span `needzero` flag like Go does, because our reback
// is COLD (the scavenger's two-epoch grace means only genuinely idle spans ever get
