// setByte(self, i, value) — store a single byte at byte-offset `i`. Bound:
// 0 <= i < length * element_size. COWs the buffer first so rdata-backed
// MMs become writable. Throws invalidByteRange (wire 7).
function __managed_mem_set_byte(self MachineWord, i BufferCoordinate, value MachineWord) returns MachineWord throws __ManagedMemoryError
	if i < 0 'lo'
		throw __ManagedMemoryError.invalidByteRange
	end 'lo'
	// Byte writes are bounded by CAPACITY (the allocated region), NOT length —
	// mirroring `__managed_mem_set` (the element setter, which gates on capacity).
	// `create` leaves the record at length 0 / capacity = count, and the
	// create-then-setByte-then-setLength idiom (`Character.fromCodepoint`, string
	// building) writes into freshly-allocated slots BEFORE `setLength` publishes
	// them; a length gate would reject every such write (0 >= length*elemSize).
	// The C# runtime avoids this only because its `create` seeds length = count;
	// the self-hosted model seeds length 0 and gates every setter on capacity, so
