// remove(self, idx) — load the element at idx, shift the tail left by one
// element via a byte-by-byte ascending loop (overlap-safe), erase the slot the
// shift vacated at the top, decrement length, and return the loaded value.
// Throws indexOutOfBounds (wire 1).
//
// NO element teardown runs here: the removed element is HANDED OUT to the caller
// (`Array.remove` / `Array.pop` return it), so its +1 moves with it. But the
// tail-shift leaves the OLD last slot holding a duplicate of the element now at
// length-2 — and a `pop` (idx == length-1) leaves it holding the very pointer it
// just gave away. Either way the slot sits above the new length holding a pointer
// this record no longer owns, so it must be erased: without that, a `resize` back
// over it re-adopts a pointer that is already owned elsewhere (or already freed),
// and the teardown walk decrefs it a second time. This is the same
// zero-the-vacated-slot rule as clear/shrink — see THE CAPACITY-SLOT INVARIANT.
